Surrogate-based pooled virtual refinement
=========================================

Enable #UsePooledVirtual in Cards/FKS_params.dat to collect and refine the
iteration-local VirtTrickSurrogate. Pooling is optional and defaults to false.
Mode 1 or 2 of VirtTrickSurrogate supplies the baseline; requesting mode 0 with
pooling selects mode 2. Pooling disables the older UsePolyVirtual option.

Collection and freeze
---------------------
The native surrogate keeps learning after each grid iteration, using the
Born, cached colour links and Born-pair logarithms. MINT continues learning
its fallback alongside it. Workers save only virtuals already evaluated by
the native loop sampler. MC@NLO collects in step 0 and fixed order in step 1.
No worker communicates during a stage and no extra loop labels are requested.

Each record contains the exact reconstructed sampled virtual, its native
Born carrier, loop-call probability, current subtraction, physical surrogate
inputs, invariant inputs, actual coupling factors, integration coordinates,
iteration and channel, native helicity map, selected helicity/probability,
complete Born proposal and momenta. MC records also retain fully folded
S-event flavour weights with both virtual pieces removed. Native checks
verify the flavour distribution against the approximation and residual
records before marking those event groups as supported.

All PDF, bias, integration and native helicity factors remain in the targets.
Only the fixed-order split-replica bookkeeping factor WGT_MULT is stripped.
Training weights undo selective loop calls and normalize each integration
batch by its committed attempted-point exposure, including failed cuts.
Rejected iterations never supply labels. Targets are absolute weighted
virtuals; neither V/B nor an additional helicity reweighting is fitted.
The stored causal subtraction reconstructs V and is not a residual label.

At the existing global barrier the controller trains, in this order:

  1. The same physical surrogate basis, with process-shared coefficients and
     ridge-regularized channel deviations. The fit carries the runtime extra
     QCD/QED loop coupling. Colour-link inputs have their extra g**2 removed.
  2. A linear, then compact quadratic fit to the remaining residual. Known
     cusp/Coulomb/velocity inputs stay linear. Simpler fits are retained
     when they are comparably accurate.
  3. Small native-helicity outputs trained on the remaining residual. A shared
     model uses Born helicity fractions as inputs; rare helicities retain the
     common prediction. A nonlinear candidate is considered only if useful.

Recipes, blends, input bounds, coefficients and head eligibility are fitted
without later validation labels. Three chronological splits keep whole
iterations and all orders of a point together. Four committed iterations are
needed in a contributing worker/channel history; shorter histories supply
training observations. The local physical surrogate and MINT comparator are
refitted using the same earlier labels. The baseline and residual are both
refitted inside conditional validation. Chosen coefficients are then refitted
on all committed observations, with the validated head eligibility frozen.

Fixed-order promotion prioritizes residual second moments. MC promotion uses
physical residual L1 with RMS guards to reduce residual sampling work, then
checks the combined negative weight of the fully folded S flavours and the
separate virtual remainder. Coupling orders are summed before taking signs.
Conditional checks use the probability-weighted prediction for S events and
the actual selected prediction for the residual. H-event weights are
unchanged and cancel in this paired comparison. Unsupported changed event
groups reject promotion. This is a capture-based promotion guard, not an
independent measurement of production efficiency or CPU per effective event.

All selected coefficients, channel choices and helicity corrections are frozen
before envelope calibration, including deferred-loop proposals. Exact
residuals are retained everywhere. Out-of-domain points request exact loops.
Failed fits or rejected refinements retain the saved native surrogate and
its grid fallback. Without NumPy no pooled model is installed; native
iteration adaptation continues. Compact conditional linear solves can use
NumPy alone; large sparse and nonlinear fits require optional SciPy.

Native helicity sampling
------------------------
PickHelicityMC still makes the only native helicity draw using Born weights,
including representative multiplicities and the mixture of Born squared
orders at the Ellis-Sexton scale. If Y_h is the already reweighted native
virtual estimator and A_h is its prediction, then

  A_mean = sum_h q_h A_h
  corrected = A_mean + I/p * (Y_h - A_h).

A full-sum branch subtracts A_mean. Collection, MINT adaptation and deferred
loops reconstruct the exact virtual using the actual selected subtraction.
Inference evaluates cheap model outputs for the mean without additional
helicity draws, loop calls or random numbers. Both selected and mean
predictions remain available in the saved deferred context.

Files and compatibility
-----------------------
New native captures use VIRTUAL_POOL_SAMPLES 5, catalog 9. After the ordinary
particle/colour metadata the header stores the surrogate feature count, then
the complete native helicity table. Each row extends the version-4 prefix
with g**2, a surrogate-valid flag and the dimensionless physical basis. Its
conditional suffix is followed by the S-flavour count, nonvirtual S weights
and the corresponding normalized flavour fractions. Zero groups mean that
no supported MC event replay is available (including fixed-order captures).

New models use VIRTUAL_POOL_MODEL 4. Each order retains its polynomial
residual records followed by SURROGATE nfeatures nchannels, the shared basis
coefficients, then channel ID, activation blend and deviation coefficients.
A zero-size basis is an explicit native fallback. These records are part of
the same model file/checksum; there is no separately transferable baseline.
Optional virtual_pool_neural.dat uses companion format 4, with additive
helicity residuals. The reserved context table contains the identity only;
the reserved head-interpretation field is -1.

VSU rows persist the local iteration fit in mint_grids even with pooling.
Model and companion checksums, generated amplitudes, Born/helicity resources,
physics cards and runtime sources are bound by manifest version 3 for resumes.
Changing the approximation or its physics inputs requires fresh grid setup
and envelope calibration. Generation-only changes to event counts and seeds
remain supported. Controller and worker transfers use the existing model,
companion and mode files.

Python inference accepts the same surrogate inputs via the optional
surrogate, channels and supported arguments of virtual_pool.predict and
virtual_pool.helicity.predict_model. As for catalog 9 previously, born is the
coupling-dressed weighted carrier and mint is the native weighted baseline.
The surrogate argument is the dimensionless basis with g**2 removed from its
three dipole entries; channels contains the native integration-channel IDs.

Implementation:
  virtual_surrogate.f90       native iteration-local basis and fits
  virtual_features.f90        invariant, colour and helicity input kernels
  virtual_approximation.f     weighted observations and deferred native loops
  virtual_pooled.f90          capture, frozen inference and helicity bookkeeping
  virtual_pool/__init__.py   controller training and public entry points
  virtual_pool/data.py       versions, strict codecs and sample normalization
  virtual_pool/validation.py point grouping, splits and residual diagnostics
  virtual_pool/surrogate.py  shared/channel fits and joint event promotion
  virtual_pool/polynomial.py compact residual regression and MINT replay
  virtual_pool/helicity.py   conditional residual fits and portable inference

The Python package lives in madgraph/various and is copied intact into
bin/internal in generated processes. Imports of virtual_pool.train_pool,
virtual_pool.load_model and virtual_pool.predict retain their existing API.
The virtual_pool_neural.dat companion filename is unchanged.

Supported formats
-----------------
The pooled implementation accepts capture version 5, common-model version 4
and additive companion version 4. Older pooled artifacts require fresh grid
setup and training. The standalone UsePolyVirtual/polfit option remains
available independently of pooling.
